      <div set:html={`
<p>On September 15, 2026, Onyx International announced the <strong>BOOX&nbsp;Palma&nbsp;3</strong> &mdash; the third generation of the phone-shaped ePaper reader that has, since 2023, defined the entire pocket-e-reader category. The Palma 3 ships with a 4&nbsp;nm octa-core processor, 6&nbsp;GB of RAM, 128&nbsp;GB of storage, Android 16 out of the box, and a refined anodized aluminum chassis with a paper-textured fiberglass rear panel. It is the first Palma to ship with active stylus support, and the first BOOX device ever to launch on Android 16 day-and-date.</p>

<p>Listed on <a href="https://shop.boox.com/products/palma3" rel="noopener">shop.boox.com/products/palma3</a> at <strong>US$339.99</strong> (US SKU <code>Palma3setus</code>) / US$319.99 (Hong Kong SKU <code>Palma3setcn</code>), the Palma 3 is currently listed as <em>Sold Out</em> in both regions &mdash; a strong early signal for a device Boox has not yet put on a firm release-date schedule. The two larger devices Boox announced alongside it (the Note&nbsp;Air6&nbsp;C and the Note&nbsp;Mini&nbsp;C) are the focus of our separate Note&nbsp;Air6&nbsp;C coverage; this guide is Palma 3 only.</p>

<p>If you read <strong>EPUB</strong> on a phone-sized device today, this is the upgrade you have been waiting for since the <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">original Palma guide</a> in 2024 and the <a href="/blog/boox-picco-ifa-2026-linux-100-dollar-price/">Picco&rsquo;s $100 Linux debut</a> earlier this month. The Palma 3 is the first Palma to step out of the experimental pocket category and into the &ldquo;small, capable primary reader&rdquo; slot.</p>

<h2 id="what-changed">What Actually Changed From the Palma 2</h2>

<p>The shape is familiar: 159 &times; 78 &times; 7.8&nbsp;mm, 6.13-inch ePaper display, capacitive touch, microSD slot, USB-C with OTG, a 3,950&nbsp;mAh battery. Boox has not invented a new form factor; it has refined the one that already worked. The changes that matter for daily EPUB reading are concentrated in five areas:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Processor &amp; memory.</strong> A 4&nbsp;nm octa-core CPU with Boox Super Refresh (BSR) acceleration, paired with 6&nbsp;GB of RAM and 128&nbsp;GB of internal storage. The Palma 2 topped out at 6&nbsp;GB/128&nbsp;GB on storage but used a 12&nbsp;nm chip. The new SoC is the single biggest performance jump in the line&rsquo;s history and the first time Boox has used a sub-7&nbsp;nm part in a pocket device.</li>
  <li><strong>Android 16.</strong> Out of the box, with Google Play Store. The Palma 2 launched on Android 13; the new model skips 14 and 15 entirely. This is a notable move because the public Android 16 release is only one generation ahead of where most flagship e-ink tablets sit.</li>
  <li><strong>Stylus support.</strong> The first Palma to accept active stylus input. Boox has not bundled a pen in the standard box, but the capacitive layer now recognizes a third-party active stylus for notes, margin annotation, and highlighting in EPUBs that support it.</li>
  <li><strong>Chassis.</strong> Anodized aluminum frame (replacing the polycarbonate frame on the Palma 2) and a fiberglass rear panel with a paper-textured finish. Same 195&nbsp;g weight; same 7.8&nbsp;mm thickness. The IP rating is still not advertised &mdash; treat it as a dry-hands device, not a beach device.</li>
  <li><strong>Camera &amp; light sensor.</strong> A 16&nbsp;MP rear camera with LED flash (for document scanning, persisted from the Palma 2) plus a dedicated ambient light sensor to drive the front-light&rsquo;s warm/cold CTM adjustment. The Smart Button and fingerprint-in-power-button combo are unchanged.</li>
</ol>

<p>What has <em>not</em> changed: the 300&nbsp;PPI resolution, the 6.13-inch screen size, the 3,950&nbsp;mAh battery, the microSD slot, the USB-C port, and the page-turn/volume buttons. The Palma 3 is a hardware refinement of a working design, not a redesign.</p>

