<p>Two notes on the spec sheet. First, the &ldquo;4&nbsp;nm octa-core + BSR&rdquo; line is a vendor description: Boox does not publish the exact silicon vendor (Qualcomm vs MediaTek vs Unisoc) on the product page, and the part number is not disclosed. Treat &ldquo;4&nbsp;nm octa-core&rdquo; as a process-node claim, not a chipset guarantee. Second, the 6.13-inch diagonal is measured per Boox&rsquo;s standard ePaper rounding; the usable display area is fractionally smaller than the number implies.</p>

<h2 id="android-16">What Android 16 Actually Means for EPUB Readers</h2>

<p>Android 16 is the public release most flagship phones are still migrating to. Boox shipping it on a $340 e-reader on day one is unusual and worth a short explanation of what it changes for the user who only cares about reading.</p>

<p>For EPUB specifically, three things matter:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Native EPUB3 + reflow support.</strong> Boox&rsquo;s NeoReader and the bundled reading apps pick up Android 16&rsquo;s updated system font stack and improved text-rendering pipeline. In practice, complex EPUB3 files (fixed-layout picture books, mathML, vertical CJK, media overlays) render with fewer fallbacks than on Android 13.</li>
  <li><strong>Background app hygiene.</strong> Android 16 tightens background-process limits, which for a reading device means longer battery life when the reader is sitting idle with Wi-Fi on. Anecdotally, Boox&rsquo;s own page-turn engine is more responsive after the change because fewer system services compete for CPU time.</li>
  <li><strong>Google Play compatibility.</strong> Android 16 ships with the most recent Play Integrity API, which means a wider range of reading apps (Kindle, Kobo, Libby, Audible, Pocket, Instapaper) install and authenticate without the &ldquo;device not certified&rdquo; warning that occasionally surfaces on older Boox devices.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of this is exclusive to the Palma 3. The same Android 16 build will roll out to the Note Air6&nbsp;C and the Note Mini&nbsp;C. What is exclusive is the timing: the Palma 3 is the first Boox device to <em>launch</em> on Android 16, which means early buyers will get the latest Play Store compatibility without waiting for an OTA.</p>

<h2 id="stylus">Stylus Support: What It Adds to EPUB Reading</h2>

<p>This is the headline change for anyone who has been using the Palma 2 as a note-adjacent device. The capacitive layer now recognizes an active stylus (Wacom-compatible AES, though Boox does not list a specific pen model on the product page), which opens three EPUB-relevant workflows that the older Palma could not do:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Margin annotation in EPUBs.</strong> EPUB3 supports SVG-based annotations. A stylus tap on a word drops a highlight; a longer press opens a text-input bubble for a margin note. This is the same workflow you get on the Boox Tab and Note lines, and it is genuinely useful for non-fiction reading where you want to come back to a passage.</li>
  <li><strong>Handwritten sticky notes.</strong> Long-press anywhere in a non-DRMed EPUB to open a handwriting overlay. Notes are saved per-page and exported as PNG or PDF.</li>
  <li><strong>Underline + strikethrough without the on-screen keyboard.</strong> The capacitive keyboard on a 6.13-inch screen is fine for short search queries but not for paragraph-long highlights. The stylus is faster and less error-prone.</li>
</ol>

<p>For DRM-locked EPUBs from Amazon, Apple Books, or Kobo, the stylus works the same as the rest of the system &mdash; the reader app handles annotation in its own database, and export to the EPUB annotation spec depends on whether the source file allows it (most Kobo and Kindle EPUBs do, in practice). The stylus does not bypass DRM; it just makes the annotation step on the device less fiddly.</p>

<h2 id="price-positioning">Price, Positioning, and the Pocket-Reader Landscape</h2>

<p>At US$339.99, the Palma 3 sits above the XTEINK X4 Classic ($79, <a href="/blog/xteink-x4-classic-79-pocket-epub-reader-2026/">covered here</a>) and the Boox Picco ($109.99) but below the original Palma 2 ($269.99) and the Boox Note Air5&nbsp;C ($549.99). That is a meaningful jump from the $269 Palma 2 launch price, and the new model is the most expensive pocket-format Boox has ever shipped.</p>

<p>What you are paying for, line by line:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>+$70 vs. the Palma 2:</strong> the 4&nbsp;nm SoC, the anodized frame, the fiberglass rear, Android 16, and the stylus layer.</li>
  <li><strong>+$230 vs. the XTEINK X4 Classic:</strong> a full Android 16 app ecosystem (the X4 runs a custom RTOS with no Play Store), a fingerprint reader, an ambient light sensor, a document-scanning camera, stereo microphones, and active stylus support. The X4 still wins on weight (68&nbsp;g) and battery days (it lasts longer because the screen is 4.3" and the OS is not Android).</li>
  <li><strong>+$230 vs. the Boox Picco:</strong> full Android vs. the Picco&rsquo;s Linux, the stylus, the camera, the fingerprint reader. The Picco is the right pick for someone who only needs an offline EPUB reader; the Palma 3 is for someone who needs a phone-shaped reader that can also run Kindle, Kobo, and Libby side by side.</li>
</ul>

<p>The Palma 3 is also the only Boox pocket device shipping with stylus support in 2026. If stylus-driven margin annotation in EPUB3 is your primary reason for carrying a phone-sized reader, the Palma 3 is currently the only option from the Boox catalog at this size.</p>

<h2 id="battery">Battery, Charging, and the Same 3,950&nbsp;mAh Story</h2>

<p>Battery capacity has not changed: 3,950&nbsp;mAh, the same cell the original Palma shipped with in 2023. What has changed is the power profile. The 4&nbsp;nm SoC draws meaningfully less idle current than the 12&nbsp;nm chip in the Palma 2, and Android 16&rsquo;s tighter background-process limits reduce wake-locks. The 9to5Google announcement note (Andrew Romero, 2026-09-15) flagged the unchanged capacity but the upgraded chipset as the trade-off; expect roughly 5&ndash;7 days of mixed EPUB reading per charge, versus the 3&ndash;4 days a Palma 2 returns in the same use case. Real-world battery tests will land once review units ship; treat that estimate as a working number, not a Boox-published spec.</p>

<p>Charging is still USB-C, no wireless charging, no fast-charge protocol. A full 0&rarr;100% charge takes about 90 minutes with a 10&nbsp;W adapter. The microSD slot, fingerprint-in-power-button, Smart Button, and dual microphones are all unchanged from the Palma 2 hardware blueprint.</p>

<h2 id="availability">Availability, SKUs, and the &ldquo;Sold Out&rdquo; Question</h2>

<p>The Palma 3 is currently listed on the official Boox store as <em>Sold Out</em> in both the US ($339.99) and Hong Kong ($319.99) SKUs. Boox has not announced a release date for the Palma 3 as of the September 15 announcement. The Note Air6&nbsp;C, by contrast, is <em>in stock and shipping today</em> &mdash; Boox specifically called out the Note Air6&nbsp;C as the only one of the three announced devices available starting September 15.</p>

<p>What &ldquo;sold out&rdquo; on day one of an unannounced release date actually means: Boox has listed the product page, opened a pre-order or reservation pipeline, and the early allocation has already been claimed by the first wave of Palma-line enthusiasts. If you want one, the practical move is to (a) sign up for the Boox newsletter for the restock notice, (b) check authorized resellers (Good e-Reader, MyDeepGuide, the Boox Discord), and (c) keep an eye on the EU and UK storefronts, which sometimes open with separate stock.</p>

<p>Pricing is valid through <code>priceValidUntil: 2027-09-15</code> per the JSON-LD <code>Offer</code> block, so the US$339.99 number is anchored for a year regardless of currency conversion drift.</p>

<h2 id="who-for">Who the Palma 3 Is For</h2>

<p>The Palma 3 is the right pick if you want one device that does all of the following without compromises:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Fits in a back pocket (159 &times; 78 &times; 7.8&nbsp;mm, 195&nbsp;g).</li>
  <li>Runs Kindle, Kobo, Libby, and Audible side by side without an Android-version warning.</li>
  <li>Reads EPUBs natively with a real capacitive touchscreen, not a stylus-first UI.</li>
  <li>Annotates EPUBs with a stylus when you want to mark up a passage.</li>
  <li>Scans paper documents to searchable PDF using the rear 16&nbsp;MP camera.</li>
  <li>Holds a microSD card full of sideloaded EPUBs and runs for a work-week on a charge.</li>
</ul>

<p>It is the wrong pick if you read mostly in fixed-layout PDFs (the 6.13" screen is too small for academic papers in A4/Letter format) or if you do not need the Play Store at all &mdash; in which case the $110 <a href="/blog/boox-picco-ifa-2026-linux-100-dollar-price/">Boox Picco</a> or the $79 XTEINK X4 Classic covers your use case at a third of the price and double the battery life.</p>

